WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.590
Hong Kong publishes both wastewater
and clinical COVID-19 monitoring data.

2
00:00:06.078 --> 00:00:12.075
When the signals move differently, researchers need to know
whether wastewater adds useful information about what comes

3
00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:15.750
next. Our proposed product tests a specific question:

4
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:22.775
can wastewater improve forecasts of clinical test
positivity beyond forecasts using clinical history

5
00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:28.930
alone? The focus is measurable added predictive
value for this particular monitoring task.

6
00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:36.410
The intended users are public health researchers
and research assistants in Hong Kong.

7
00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.110
They need to compare signals, examine unusual weeks,

8
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.000
and explain how reliable a forecast is.

9
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.080
The tool brings these steps into one workflow:

10
00:00:46.310 --> 00:00:50.650
choose a historical week, compare
predictions, inspect the evidence,

11
00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.180
and export the results.

12
00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.210
This supports transparent review without
rebuilding the analysis each time.

13
00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:04.190
The main source is the Department
of Health's COVID-19 Express dataset on

14
00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:10.290
data.gov.hk. The current snapshot contains
one hundred and ninety-four weekly

15
00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:13.560
records from 2023 to 2026.

16
00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:18.940
It combines clinical specimen positivity
with the official wastewater indicator.

17
00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:22.740
Two wastewater weeks are missing and remain missing.

18
00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:27.650
The prediction target is the proportion
of tested specimens that are positive.

19
00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:33.750
A working Python prototype already provides trend charts,

20
00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:38.850
historical replay, model comparison,
and unusual-pattern exploration.

21
00:01:39.410 --> 00:01:43.780
Ridge regression compares clinical history
with the same history plus wastewater.

22
00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:50.850
K-means groups similar signal combinations, while Local
Outlier Factor highlights unusual observations for

23
00:01:50.870 --> 00:01:56.700
review. These methods are implemented with NumPy,
so their calculations can be examined,

24
00:01:56.990 --> 00:01:59.890
explained and reproduced from saved inputs.

25
00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:06.780
The primary forecast is two monitoring
weeks after the latest observed week,

26
00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:09.150
with actual target dates displayed.

27
00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:12.000
Models are compared on the same test weeks,

28
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.080
with settings selected earlier.

29
00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:18.830
The current retrospective evaluation uses a frozen snapshot.

30
00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:23.420
Initial gains depend on prediction
bounds, and uncertainty remains.

31
00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:29.530
The next evaluation will reconstruct historical
data availability and examine revisions.

32
00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:37.510
The intended impact is a more transparent way to assess
the value of Hong Kong's wastewater monitoring

33
00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:43.790
data. Researchers could use the tool
to identify questions worth investigating and

34
00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.040
judge when an extra signal helps.

35
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:50.590
Next steps include user feedback and clearer explanations.

36
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.290
A useful outcome is also learning
when wastewater adds little value,

37
00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.060
with the evidence available for inspection.
